r of his  own volition to get threats off you he's  a good lad are you watch out  ultimately although these issues can be  irksome they are small and in the grand  scheme of things don't drastically  impact how the mechanics coalesce combat  is much pacier and more Dynamic than  before and it is thrilling regardless of  whether you're fighting a smattering of  draggers or trading blows with Gods it's  thoughtfully designed and thoroughly  satisfying from the first swing of the X  to the very last  Kratos and atreus's battles take them  across all nine Realms which is a change  from the previous game where two of the  Realms were locked away one of the most  interesting parts of God of War Ragnarok  is how it reimagines areas that were in  the original game thimblewinter is used  as a narrative conceit to give these  familiar locales New Life in Norse  mythology thimble winter which  translates to The Long Winter serves as  the Prelude to Ragnarok in game it  affects the nine Realms in different  ways midgard for example is besieged by  a bitter cold so when you return to the  Lake of the nine once again it has  completely frozen over key areas of the  lake that you may have become familiar  with in the last game May now be  inaccessible as new parts of it open up  a cold wind sweeps across the icy  surface of the lake and at the center of  it all tears Temple is now barely  recognizable under the buildup of snow  in the distance icy mountain peaks serve  as the backdrop to a single lightning  strike Frozen in place as the mark of an  intense battle naturally navigation is  completely changed since you can no  longer sail the waters instead Kratos  and atreus get around on a sled that is  pulled by their trusty pet walls which  is a nice way to mix up the way you get  around at certain points you can even  use their Keen sense of awareness to  sniff out objectives which is a nice  touch spartal fine by comparison looks  picturesque from the outskirts with its  bright blue skies but as you venture  deeper into its Wetlands it becomes more  desert-like with craggy rocks awkwardly  jutting out and collapse wooden mining  equipment filling the spaces in between  there's an unpleasant arid atmosphere to  it and yet at the same time it's quite  the visual spectacle to behold rich in  detail and intricate in its construction  vanheim home of the Vanier is a verdant  Forest that has reclaimed much of the  landscape trees gently Sway in the wind  Moss has settled Atop The Water and  vegetation snakes along Stone floors and  up onto man-made shrines and if you take  a moment to stop and look around you'll  find Wildlife nestled amongst all the  green it's a place that is very alive  which makes it a nice contrast to the  coldness of midgar or the glistening  Majesty of alfheim's architecture there  are other areas I won't spoil some of  which have a good amount to see and do  While others serve a specific function  no one area in God of War Ragnarok comes  across as big as the lake of nine but it  seems like this is a symptom of how  you're constantly moving around a  variety of locations as opposed to  repeatedly returning to one regardless  each realm has a grand sense of scale  and as you move between them the scope  of the world as a whole far eclipses  what came before while the main story  campaign moves Kratos and the trails  through all nine Realms there are plenty  of side quests to undertake in each of  these locations yet another Testament to  the writing is that these quests are  never a distraction and always present a  good reward whether that be an item or  crafting materials at the very least  however most of the time they also  deliver some piece of law further flesh  out aside character or give Kratos a  trace and mimir something new to bond  over it is done if you could call so me  got a task done a wee draw part of the  Asgardian occupational bucket going  anywhere with these characters is an  exciting prospect and I often found  myself just wandering around to let  conversations play out or hoping that  new ones would start in most cases they  did and I was treated to another  valuable character growth moment in  Ragnarok Kratos is now more inquisitive  memir is always ready and willing to  offer his insight and atreus takes the  opportunity to make jokes at both their  expense and that's something I  definitely didn't expect God of War  Ragnarok is a funny game there are more  than a few laugh out loud moments that  endeared me to this new family unit that  developed over the last game and  flourishes in this one is everything  okay okay hi old friend just looting on  your chair we'll be right along my dad  likes Luke and that's what distinguishes  God of all Ragnarok from his predecessor  and most other action games despite  being a story about warring gods and the  end of the world the soul of the game is  something far more sentimental you may  have noticed that I've avoided  mentioning the names of characters or  describing plot beats and that was  intentional to talk more about anything  or anyone would be to rob you of some of  the most unexpected things that God of  War Ragnarok achieved so with this  narrative and the themes they explore  the last game's thoughtful approach to  exploring fatherhood was unexpected but  Ragnarok somehow manages to feel like an  even more personal tale about the  complicated nature of families and the  people that make them up for every  moment of brutality there's one of  genuine and relatable emotion how they  land will vary from person to person but  there are multiple that left me with  tears welling up if nothing else God of  War Ragnarok further cements Sony Santa  Monica's narrative team as one one of  the best in the business  [Music]  [Music]
 

